package pages

// TermsOfService and PrivacyPolicy describe this specific product (a
// multi-tenant SaaS for LPG plants processing real payments via
// Paystack) rather than generic placeholder legal text. Real content,
// but not a substitute for actual legal review before this product
// handles real customer money at any real scale -- that review is a
// step for the user to take separately, not something a generated page
// can stand in for.

templ TermsOfService() {
	@marketingPageShell("Terms of Service | GatsToGo", termsContent())
}

templ termsContent() {
	<main class="article-layout">
		<div class="section-eyebrow">Legal</div>
		<h1>Terms of Service</h1>
		<p class="updated">Last updated: { legalUpdatedDate }</p>

		<p>These terms govern use of GatsToGo, a software platform that lets an LPG (gas) plant business ("Plant", "you") sell gas online, manage staff and shifts, and give its own customers ("End Users") a way to buy gas and look up past purchases. By creating a plant on GatsToGo or using a plant's page as an End User, you agree to these terms.</p>

		<h2>What GatsToGo provides</h2>
		<p>A branded page for a Plant's End Users to buy gas, a staff terminal for redeeming and fulfilling orders, and a dashboard for the Plant to track sales, cash, and staff activity. GatsToGo is not itself a gas retailer. The Plant is solely responsible for fulfilling orders, pricing accurately, and complying with any applicable regulations for selling LPG in its jurisdiction.</p>

		<h2>Accounts and access</h2>
		<p>A Plant's owner account is created either through self-service sign-up (reviewed before activation) or by our team directly. The Plant is responsible for the accuracy of the information it provides, for keeping owner and staff login credentials confidential, and for any activity carried out under its account.</p>

		<h2>Payments</h2>
		<p>End User payments are processed by Paystack, a third-party payment processor. GatsToGo does not collect or store full card details. That data is handled entirely by Paystack. A Plant is responsible for any fees Paystack charges on transactions processed through its page.</p>

		<h2>Plant responsibilities</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>Fulfilling orders that customers have paid for, in good faith and within a reasonable time.</li>
			<li>Keeping listed prices accurate and up to date.</li>
			<li>Restricting staff terminal access (PINs) to people actually authorized to redeem and fill orders on the Plant's behalf.</li>
			<li>Not using GatsToGo for any purpose other than the sale and fulfillment of LPG gas to its own End Users.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>Service availability</h2>
		<p>We aim to keep GatsToGo available and reliable, but this is an early-stage product provided on a best-effort basis without an uptime guarantee at this stage. We'll do our best to give notice of planned maintenance that might affect a Plant's page.</p>

		<h2>Suspension and termination</h2>
		<p>We may suspend or close a Plant's account if it violates these terms, is used fraudulently, or if a plant becomes inactive for an extended period. A Plant may request closure of its account at any time by contacting us.</p>

		<h2>Limitation of liability</h2>
		<p>GatsToGo is provided "as is." To the fullest extent permitted by law, GatsToGo is not liable for indirect, incidental, or consequential damages arising from use of the platform, including losses related to payment processing delays or disputes between a Plant and its End Users.</p>

		<h2>Changes to these terms</h2>
		<p>We may update these terms as the product evolves. Continued use of GatsToGo after a change means you accept the updated terms.</p>

		<h2>Contact</h2>
		<p>Questions about these terms can be sent to the contact address on our <a href="/about">About page</a>.</p>
	</main>
}

templ PrivacyPolicy() {
	@marketingPageShell("Privacy Policy | GatsToGo", privacyContent())
}

templ privacyContent() {
	<main class="article-layout">
		<div class="section-eyebrow">Legal</div>
		<h1>Privacy Policy</h1>
		<p class="updated">Last updated: { legalUpdatedDate }</p>

		<p>This describes what information GatsToGo collects and how it's used, for both Plants (the gas businesses using GatsToGo) and their End Users (the customers who buy gas through a Plant's page).</p>

		<h2>What we collect</h2>
		<ul>
			<li><strong>Plant owners and staff:</strong> name, phone number, role, and a securely hashed password or PIN (we never store these in plain text).</li>
			<li><strong>End Users:</strong> phone number, and purchase records: the amount, size, timestamp, and payment channel of each order. We do not require End Users to create an account.</li>
			<li><strong>Payment information:</strong> handled entirely by Paystack. GatsToGo never sees or stores full card numbers.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>How we use it</h2>
		<ul>
			<li>To operate the service: processing orders, running the staff terminal, and showing a Plant its own sales and cash data.</li>
			<li>To verify an End User's identity when looking up a past receipt, by sending a one-time code to the phone number they provide.</li>
			<li>To detect and prevent fraud or abuse of the platform.</li>
		</ul>

		<h2>What we don't do</h2>
		<p>We don't sell personal data to third parties. We don't share an End User's information across different Plants. Each Plant only sees the purchases made on its own page.</p>

		<h2>Data sharing</h2>
		<p>Payment data is shared with Paystack, as required to process a transaction. We don't share data with any other third party except where required by law.</p>

		<h2>Data retention</h2>
		<p>We keep purchase and account records for as long as a Plant's account is active, and for a reasonable period afterward for accounting and legal purposes.</p>

		<h2>Your rights</h2>
		<p>You can ask us what personal data we hold about you, or ask us to correct or delete it, subject to what we're legally required to keep (such as transaction records). Contact us using the details on our <a href="/about">About page</a>.</p>

		<h2>Changes to this policy</h2>
		<p>We may update this policy as the product evolves. We'll update the date at the top of this page when we do.</p>
	</main>
}

// legalUpdatedDate is a plain literal, not time.Now() -- these pages
// should only report a new date when their content actually changes, not
// on every deploy.
const legalUpdatedDate = "August 2026"
